ility of r  given  g for each of these  configurations so now i have to actually  instantiate all possible configurations  i'm going to load a one  into each of these counts  and then i'm going to look at my  training data  and i'm going to add two there's two d4s  one d5 one c1 and one c5  now given these counts i'm going to  normalize to get my probability limit  look at all the d's count them up  normalize i get  um some  of these here and look at all the case  rows where g c  i'm going to  sum in  so now  what we revisit our probability estimate  of r equals two given uh g equals c  this was zero before  but now it's uh  here that's one over seven which is  greater than zero  so now because we smooth these estimates  now we have a little bit more  probability on even  those outcomes that we've never seen  during training  the key idea behind maximum likelihood  laplace smoothing is follows  so we're going to go through each  distribution  and partial assignment  uh to  the parents of a node and the node  itself  and we're simply going to add lambda  to the count  now we do maximum likelihood estimation  as usual so we're going to go through  the training data and increment those  counts based on what we saw  and then we count and normalize and  that's it  so the interpretation that we can place  on the plot smoothing is it's like we  hallucinated  lambda occurrences of each local  assignment so sometimes these uh lambda  accounts are called pseudo counts  because they're not based on the data  they're kind of made up for virtual  counts  so you can think about pretending you  saw some examples before you saw data  and then do a maximum likelihood image  so  how  much  should lambda be how much smoothing  should we have and how does it interact  with the um there's two observations i  want to make first is that the more  you smooth which means that the more the  bigger the lambda is  the closer you're going to push the  probability estimates closer to the  uniform distribution so for example  if i just smooth with lambda equals  one-half  then and i observe only a d here then  the probabilities estimates are going to  be three quarters in one corner whereas  if i smooth with one then the  probabilities are going to be two-thirds  and one-third which is closer to  half-half  the second observation i want to make is  that no matter what you set lambda to  the data wins out in the end  so suppose we only see examples of  dramas  so suppose that we're smoothing with  lambda equals one and we saw only one  example of g equals  then again the problem estimates are  two-thirds and one-third  but suppose we keep on seeing dramas  over and over again so we see 998 of  them  and now if we account and  normalize then we get the probability  estimate as 0.999  uh  of drama which is much closer to  scene-only dramas  so to summarize  uh we  looked at laplace smoothing for avoiding  overfitting and estimating beijing  networks  and the key idea is that  we preload counts with a lambda  and then we're going to go to training  data and we add  counts based on our data and then we  normalize  so  the smoothing pulls us away  from zeros to the uniform distribution  but in the end  all the smoothing  gets washed out with more data  that's the end
 

